    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Data formats</p>
    <h1>CSV vs JSON: Which Format Should You Use?</h1>
    <p class="lead">CSV and JSON both store data as text, but they suit different jobs. CSV is a table in plain text, and JSON is a structured format that can hold nested data.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>What CSV is good at</h2>
<p>CSV is a list of rows, with commas between the values. Spreadsheets export it and import it easily, and people can read it at a glance. It works best for flat tables with one value per cell.</p>
<h2>What JSON is good at</h2>
<p>JSON stores named fields and can nest objects and lists inside each other. Web apps and APIs use it because programs can read it directly. It handles data that does not fit neatly into rows.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes when converting</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Commas inside values:</strong> a value such as Ravi, Jr. must be wrapped in double quotes in CSV. A converter that ignores quotes will split it into two fields.</li>
<li><strong>Types:</strong> every CSV value is text. The number 34 becomes the text "34" in JSON unless the converter or your code changes it.</li>
<li><strong>Empty cells:</strong> decide whether an empty cell becomes an empty string or null, and keep the choice consistent.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/csv-to-json.html">CSV to JSON Converter</a> uses the first row as field names and handles quoted commas. Your data stays in your browser.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>The CSV row "Ravi, Jr.",Colombo,27 under the header name,city,age becomes &#123;"name": "Ravi, Jr.", "city": "Colombo", "age": "27"&#125;. The comma inside the quotes stays part of the name, and 27 is kept as the text "27" because CSV stores every value as text.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Can I open JSON in a spreadsheet?</summary><p>Most spreadsheets need an import step or a plug-in for nested JSON. Flat JSON arrays of objects can often be converted to CSV first.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is CSV an official standard?</summary><p>CSV is described in RFC 4180, but many programs vary in how they handle quotes, line endings and separators, so check the format before importing.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why do my numbers become text after converting?</summary><p>CSV has no types, so every value starts as text. Your code or the tool must change values such as 27 to a number if you need that.</p></details>
      </section>
